color_schemes_tester(parent, color_schemes: list)
    #parent: 
        the parent object of the app (the main window)
    #color_schemes: (list object)
        a list of either all of the color in a single color scheme or a list of lists where each inner list is a list of colors for the color scheme
            This allows you to compare colors against each other if you want to compare color schemes
    
    -makes a popup window containing the different colors in the colorscemes that are imputed

ToolTip(widget, text, color, txtColor)
    #widget:
        the object that the tool tip will show up when the mouse hovers over
    #text:
        the text that will show up when the mouse hovers over the widget
    #color:
        the color of the tool tip background (the box background)
    #txtColor:
        the color of the text inside of the tool tip

    -a helpful tool tip that helps users know what buttons and other things do

loadImages(imagePath: str, imageSize: int, multipleColorModes = False)
    #imagePath:
        This is the path to the folder that contains the images needed to be loaded
        **Must give the full path to the folder**
    #imageSize:
        the size of the image that will be created
    #multipleColorModes:
        defaults to false
            loads all the images in
        If you have multiple images, one for light color mode and one for dark color mode, you can set this to true
        **The image file names must be identical exept for '_light' or '_dark' just before the .png**
        
    -loads every image in a folder and returns a dictionary
        ------To access the Images-------
        1) load the images into a variable (this variable contains a dictionary now) *var called 'test' for this example
        2) access the image info dictionary: test[imageName] (imageName is the name of the image you are trying to access from your folder (this name excludes '_light.png' and '_dark.png'))3) access the ctkImage: test[imageName]['ctkImage'] (ctkImage is the key in the dictionary to access the ctkImage (this is constant))
        4) if you want the aspect ratio of the image (as a decimal): test[imageName]['aspectRatio'] (aspectRatio is the same as ctkImage and is constant)

    -dictionary contents
    {imageName, {'ctkImage', 'aspectRatio', 'pilLight', 'pilDark'}} 
        **pilDark is only apparent when you specify that you have light and dark mode. pilLight is the default pil**

FILEsearch (parent, searchType: str = 'open', saveType: str = '.json', fileTypes: list = [('All Files', '*.*')], startingLocation: str = '/Users/', colors: dict = {'fg': "#FFFFFF", 'hover': "#CCCCCC", 'txt': "#000000"})
    #parent:
        this is the parent window that the search window is a child of
    #searchType:
        the type of window
            ['open', 'save', 'export']
            'open': allows the user to open a file
            'save': allows the user to save (gives a place to put a file name)
            'export': works the same as save
    #saveType:
        This is the file extension of the file being saved.
        Allows for customizability
            Could use a custom file format, or a format that already exists
        **requires a . infront of your file type ('.json')**
    #fileTypes:
        here you can specify the type of files you want displayed
        Leaving this as default, displays every available file
    #startingLocation:
        where the window will open up to
        The default is the /Users/ folder
        **Requires the full path to the folder** if you want to specify a folder
    #colors:
        this is a dictionary that allows you to make a custom color scheme for the window.
        Depending on the color theme (light or dark), the icons will be dark or light respectably.

    --The easiest way to use this window pop up is to set it equal to a variable
        fs = FILEsearch(parent_window)
    --This class creates a window that pops up and is like Finder or File explorer
        It searches the computer and shows files and folders.
        The user can click into folders, go back to previous, and select files
        By clicking the selected file again, the file becomes unselected
        A selected file is represented by a green outline around the file icon
    --When the search type is set to 'save' or 'export' there is a spot to put a file name
        The user can also create new folders in this mode

placeHolder (entry: ctk.CTkEntry, placeHolderText: str, textColor: str='#000000', function=lambda: None)
    #entry: 
        this is the CTkEntry object that you want the place holder placed in
    #placeHolderText:
        This is the text that you want displayed when the CTkEntry is empty
    #textColor:
        This is the color of the text when the placeholder is not present
        (The placeholder has a set color)
    #function:
        This function parameter is completely optional
        The function will be run if you want to use the text and perform an action right after the focus is lifted from the CTkEntry
        **This function has one parameter that will be passed when it is called, that parameter is the text that is inside of the CTkEntry**

    --This class will put a placeholder inside of a CTkEntry when custom tkinter won't do it automatically for some odd reason.
